From 2685203f06f1059952a021c062014e487eff46c5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 20:06:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] drm/histb: add the VDP v2 display engine driver

Introduce the v2 display engine driver for the cv200 and mv100 lines. It
drives one CRTC with a primary plane over the channel the DT port
selects and exposes the mv100 write-back taps as DRM writeback
connectors. The DRM device, the plane/crtc/encoder scaffolding and the
component lifetime live in a shared VDP core that the backend embeds. A
cv200 boot leaves the graphics mixers in an undefined slot order and the
GP0 CSC stage bypassed; the slots are reset to the order the fetch unit
assumes and the CSC carries the cv200 matrix before the first commit.
The CSC and the mixer background follow the colour format the bridge
negotiation settled on, the plane is scaled with the GP0 coefficient set
and placed where the core clipped it, and the plane alpha comes from the
surface when the format carries one.

On the mv100 the generic programming misses points where the channel
answers differently: the update register's bit 0 is a self-clearing
strobe that has to be polled to prove the block took the write,
VDPVERSION1 reads zero while the block is held in reset, the alpha field
is seven bits wide, and the vertical porch and sync fields are eight
against sixteen for the horizontal ones, so out-of-range timings are
rejected instead of truncated into a different picture.

Both write-back taps arm per commit: each carries its own pending flag,
completion is counted against the channel's vblank, and a job whose
block is still busy stays queued for the next commit instead of stopping
the scan.

Signed-off-by: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
